  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Widespread rainfall expected late Wednesday through \nFriday as multiple disturbances move through the area. \n\nA low pressure system currently in the Great Plains will move into \nthe Great Lakes Region on Wednesday. The accompanying upper-level \ntrough will weaken and retreat north, causing the highest chance of \nrainfall to be limited to along/west of the Blue Ridge Wednesday \nnight. Ahead of the front, southerly winds will drive in moisture \nand warmer temperatures, and with the trough moving through late \nWednesday, a few thunderstorms can not be ruled out in the western \nmountains. \n\nA brief lull then occurs on Thursday, despite the front still \nremaining off to the west, due to drier air across the area. A \nslight chance of showers remains possible, before another \ndisturbance dives down around the upper-level trough and interacts \nwith the cold front over our area Thursday night into Friday \nmorning. This will trigger redevelopment of showers and potentially \nsome thunderstorms along the frontal boundary and move east into the \nPiedmont, bringing some much needed rain to the area. \n\nRain totals will be highest in the western mountains of North \nCarolina and West Virginia from the first disturbance, and in the \neastern Piedmont with the second wave of rain. Totals in those areas \nwill be up to 1.0\", with higher amounts possible in any \nthunderstorms. The I-81 corridor will essentially miss out on the \nheaviest rainfall in both waves, with totals expected to be around a \nquarter inch. Isolated flash flooding cannot be ruled out Wednesday \nnight into Thursday in the North Carolina mountains, but the risk is \nlow. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Unseasonably warm temperatures continue through \nFriday, returning to near normal this weekend. \n\nAs high pressure continues to shift offshore into the Atlantic, \nsoutherly flow will return to the Mid-Atlantic, causing temperatures \nto be well above normal for this time of year. High temperatures \nthrough the end of the week will be in the upper 60s for the \nmountains, and mid to upper 70s in the Piedmont. A few spots could \neven reach 80 degrees Wednesday and Thursday, which is about 15 \ndegrees above normal. Behind the cold front, temperatures \"cool\" \nback off to only slightly above normal, with highs mainly in the \n60s, with upper 50s for the highest elevations. \n\nOvernight lows will also be very mild through the end of the week, \nin the 50s/60s Wednesday night. By Saturday night, overnight lows \nfall into the 30s area-wide, which is around normal. This continues \nthrough the middle of next week.",
